title: 'pallet-revive: bound per-transaction receipt size'
doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    The logs of one Ethereum transaction may now take up at most 2 MiB of its receipt
    (`limits::MAX_RECEIPT_LOGS_BYTES`). The size is the RLP encoding of every log the transaction
    keeps: address, topics and data, each with its RLP headers. The limit applies to
    `eth_transact` and `eth_substrate_call`, and to dry runs and gas estimation of them, so an
    estimate reports the same outcome as inclusion. Calls that do not run as an Ethereum
    transaction, such as `Revive::call`, have no receipt and are not affected.

    A `LOG` instruction or `deposit_event` syscall that would take the receipt past the limit
    fails the emitting call frame with the new `ReceiptTooLarge` error, the same way an
    oversized event or too many topics already fail it. The frame's state changes and logs roll
    back and the calling contract can handle the failure.

    A log that the runtime mirrors on a contract's behalf outside any call frame, such as an
    ERC-20 `Transfer` for an asset balance change, cannot fail on its own. If it does not fit,
    the transaction fails with `ReceiptTooLarge` once its outermost call returns, unless the
    frame it was emitted in reverts first and takes the log with it. The transaction is included
    with a failed receipt: all its state changes roll back, no storage deposit is charged, and
    it pays the transaction fee for the weight it used, as any failed transaction does.

    Mirrored logs emitted outside any Ethereum transaction are collected into the block's
    synthetic transaction, whose receipt has the same limit. A log that does not fit there stays
    a substrate-only `ContractEmitted` event, as a log past `MaxOutsideFrameLogs` already does.

    An Ethereum transaction cannot reach this size: at the EIP-7825 limit of 2^24 gas per
    transaction, the `LOG` gas costs keep its receipt logs below 2 MiB. Contracts that stay within
    Ethereum's limits need no change.

    The RLP encoded payload of an `eth_transact` may now be at most
    `limits::MAX_TRANSACTION_PAYLOAD_SIZE` (1 MiB of code, 128 KiB of call data and 64 KiB for
    the rest of the transaction). A larger payload is rejected with
    `InvalidTransaction::ExhaustsResources` when the transaction is checked, so the transaction
    pool refuses it instead of including it and having it fail in the block. Transactions within
    the existing code and call data limits are not affected unless they also carry more than
    about 64 KiB of access or authorization lists. Dev chains that enable
    `DebugSettings::unlimited_contract_size` can no longer submit EVM init code that takes the
    payload past this limit, about 1.19 MiB.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `PrecompileExt::deposit_event` now returns `Result<(), DispatchError>`. It fails with
    `Error::ReceiptTooLarge` when the log does not fit the open receipt, and deposits nothing in
    that case. The trait is sealed, so no implementation changes; precompiles that emit logs
    through it must handle the result at the call site, normally by propagating it so the
    precompile call fails:

    ```rust
    // before
    env.deposit_event(topics, data);
    // after
    env.deposit_event(topics, data)?;
    ```

    `Error` has the new variant `ReceiptTooLarge` (index `0x45`). Code that matches on the error
    exhaustively needs an arm for it.

    `limits::MAX_TRANSACTION_PAYLOAD_SIZE` grows by 64 KiB and is now enforced on `eth_transact`
    payloads, see above. `integrity_test` sizes the Ethereum block builder from this limit and
    the receipt limit, instead of from the block weight. No storage migration and no
    configuration change is needed.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: patch
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
